From openbsd-alpha  Tue Sep 25 16:35:23 2001
From: Jay Bolton <jay () rootaction ! net>
Date: Tue, 25 Sep 2001 16:35:23 +0000
To: openbsd-alpha
Subject: Re: adaptec 2940 in Miata
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=openbsd-alpha&m=100143506011238

Thorsteinn, 

The driver for the 29xx cards is configured in the GENERIC kernel for
OpenBSD/Alpha 2.9.  This means that OpenBSD 2.9-stable should detect 
and initialize your controller, and use any devices you have attached 
to it.  This driver is *NOT* configured in the RAMDISK kernel, which 
is the kernel used in the floppy installation.  The primary reason 
for this is floppy image size constraints.

This means that without making a custom RAMDISK image you will be 
unable to access devices on the 2940 during the install, but you
will be able to use them after first boot.

Further, if you are using an older version OpenBSD/Alpha, there was at one
time a problem with some of the bus_dma changes that caused the build of
2940 drivers to fail.  At that time (GENERIC rev 1.48, 3 Jul 2000) the
decision was taken to leave this driver "not configured."  Since then,
this problem has been fixed.  The driver was added back in to the
GENERIC kernel by Nate (rev 1.60, 11 Dec 2000) after a herculean effort
by Art to make Alpha bus_dma more consistent with the rest of OpenBSD.

Jay

